From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Subject: v8: ppc64 must zero-extend loads of compressed values

Turboshaft's decompression optimization rewrites a tagged load to
result_rep = Compressed() when every use needs only the low 32 bits, and
explicitly permits such values to flow into a FrameState
(v8/src/compiler/turboshaft/decompression-optimization.cc:91 -- "The deopt code
knows how to handle compressed inputs").  On ppc64 the instruction selector
emits those loads as kPPC_LoadWordS32, i.e. lwa/plwa/lwax, which SIGN-extends.
x64 uses kX64Movl and arm64 uses kArm64LdrW; both zero-extend.

The sign-extended value is then spilled with std into a 64-bit frame slot that
the register allocator has recorded as a tagged safepoint root.  Two C++
consumers decide whether such a slot holds a compressed pointer by testing
value <= 0xFFFFFFFF:

  v8/src/execution/frames.cc:1632         VisitSpillSlot
  v8/src/deoptimizer/translated-state.cc:1851  DecompressIfNeeded

Both carry a #if V8_TARGET_ARCH_LOONG64 carve-out that also accepts is_int32(),
precisely because loong64 sign-extends the same way.  There is no ppc64 case.  So
any compressed pointer at a cage offset >= 0x80000000 fails the test, is treated
as a full pointer, and the root visitor dereferences it:

  ld    r7,0(r6)        # load the slot
  andi. r8,r7,1         # HeapObject tag?
  blelr                 # Smi -> return
  lwz   r7,-1(r7)       # untag and load the map -- SIGSEGV

Observed on POWER9 as a renderer crash during Octane 2.0 with Web Workers:
SIGSEGV, si_code 3, r7 = 0xffffffff87ee36f9, si_addr = r7-1.  The compressed
value was 0x87ee36f9 -- bit 31 set.  It reproduces only in worker isolates
because those allocate into the upper half of the 4 GB cage; the main isolate
stays below 0x80000000 and never trips it.

Fixed in the backend rather than by adding ppc64 to the two loong64 carve-outs,
so that ppc64 matches the x64/arm64 contract the C++ side is written against.
The addressing-mode downgrade is left in place: lwz is D-form and accepts every
displacement lwa does, so restricting to kInt16Imm_4ByteAligned remains correct,
merely unnecessary.

VisitWord64Sar's folded kPPC_LoadWordS32 (same file, ~line 990) is deliberately
untouched -- that one loads the high half of a 64-bit integer, where sign
extension is correct.

s390x has the identical mapping (kS390_LoadWordS32 in
instruction-selector-s390.cc) and the same missing carve-out, so it is affected
too.  Upstreamable as-is; this is not ppc64le-specific packaging.

--- a/v8/src/compiler/backend/ppc/instruction-selector-ppc.cc
+++ b/v8/src/compiler/backend/ppc/instruction-selector-ppc.cc
@@ -215,14 +215,14 @@
     case MemoryRepresentation::TaggedPointer():
       if (result_rep == RegisterRepresentation::Compressed()) {
         if (*mode != kInt34Imm) *mode = kInt16Imm_4ByteAligned;
-        return kPPC_LoadWordS32;
+        return kPPC_LoadWordU32;
       }
       DCHECK_EQ(result_rep, RegisterRepresentation::Tagged());
       return kPPC_LoadDecompressTagged;
     case MemoryRepresentation::TaggedSigned():
       if (result_rep == RegisterRepresentation::Compressed()) {
         if (*mode != kInt34Imm) *mode = kInt16Imm_4ByteAligned;
-        return kPPC_LoadWordS32;
+        return kPPC_LoadWordU32;
       }
       DCHECK_EQ(result_rep, RegisterRepresentation::Tagged());
       return kPPC_LoadDecompressTaggedSigned;
@@ -279,7 +279,7 @@
     case MachineRepresentation::kCompressed:
 #ifdef V8_COMPRESS_POINTERS
       if (*mode != kInt34Imm) *mode = kInt16Imm_4ByteAligned;
-      return kPPC_LoadWordS32;
+      return kPPC_LoadWordU32;
 #else
       UNREACHABLE();
 #endif
